[test-suite name=operator-heads ring=0
  [doc [#
# CX Conformance — operator-headed documents (I1 manifest row 8, L80/C4)
# Version: 2.0 — 2026-08-05 (the row-8 lexer fix; re-blessed per the
# ledger's flip-at-row-8 instruction, ONE re-bless for this class —
# v1.0 of this file pinned the pre-fix stringify/error bytes; the
# old→new digests are recorded in the epoch mapping file)
#
# All seven operator heads `+ - * / = < >` parse as OPERATOR-NAMED
# ELEMENTS when the single operator char is DELIMITED (followed by
# whitespace or `]`). Pre-I1, five of them silently STRINGIFIED through
# the array lane (`[+ $x 2]` → `['+ $x 2']`) and two (`*` `>`) errored
# ("expected name"). GLUED continuations keep their old routes:
# `[-1, 2]` is a negative-number array item, `[*n]` is an alias
# reference, `[+1, 2]` normalizes through the array lane.
#
# Version: 3.0 — 2026-08-25 (#976). THE ALPHABET IS NOT SEVEN AND NEVER
# WAS. v2.0 of this header closed with "Multi-char glyphs (`<=`, `**`,
# …) are NOT operator heads" — HALF RIGHT, and the wrong half was a
# content-addressing defect. The ruled head set is the EVALUATOR's
# (code/eval.v `operator_element_heads`, 18 heads); twelve are glyphs:
#
#     one-char   + * - / % = < > ~
#     two-char   != <= >=
#
# The data parser admitted only the seven above, so `%` and `<=` fell
# into the array lane and STRINGIFIED SILENTLY while `!=`, `>=` and `~`
# raised "expected name". `[<= 5 3]` therefore canonicalized to the
# very bytes of `['<= 5 3']` and shared its Tier-1 hash — two documents
# the evaluator reads differently at ONE content address (oph-301..303).
# `**` and `==` are genuinely NOT heads: they are absent from the ruled
# set, and oph-407 pins `[== 5 3]` staying in the array lane.
#
# The oph-001..010 pins below are UNMOVED by #976 — every head they
# name was already admitted. The repair shows up in oph-101..118 (the
# closed alphabet), oph-201..218 (canonical re-parse), oph-301..303
# (the collision) and oph-401..407 (the dispatch guard rails).
#
# Version: 3.1 — 2026-08-26 (#983). One route #976 MOVED without
# pinning: an operator head that runs to EOF. `op_head_delimited`
# counts end-of-input as a delimiter, so `[+` is a DELIMITED head and
# `peek_is_array_literal` sends it to the ELEMENT dispatch, where
# `parse_element` runs out of input looking for `]`. The pre-#976
# disambiguator carried an explicit `if i + 1 >= p.src.len { return
# true }` for `+ - / = <` and sent the same bytes to the ARRAY lane,
# which said "unterminated array literal". Both spellings exit rc=1, so
# nothing in the corpus noticed the swap. oph-408 pins the CURRENT
# message so the lane is a decision and not a side effect: an operator
# head stays an ELEMENT head all the way to EOF, and the diagnostic
# names the element closer it is missing.
#
# oph-409 pins the OTHER #983 finding: `[<=x]` canonicalizes to the BARE
# `[<=x]`, and that is sound rather than an emitter/parser asymmetry.
# `cx_collection_bare_safe` would refuse the string `<=x`, but that
# predicate owns map-value / sequence-item position, NOT array-item
# position — arrays answer a different question (does the image re-parse
# to the same value?) with a different predicate. It does: a GLUED
# operator glyph is not a head, so the bare image re-enters the array
# lane unchanged, and `[<=x]` shares its Tier-1 address with the quoted
# twin `['<=x']`. The emitter is deliberately NOT changed to quote —
# Tier-1 identity IS the canonical bytes, so quoting would MOVE the
# address of every array carrying such an item, and no ruling or
# migration names that move. oph-409 is the guard that keeps a future
# "tidy-up" of the emitter from making that move silently.
#]]

 [; ── The seven heads — delimited operator char = element name ── ]

 [case id=oph-001-plus-element level=core
  [tags operator-heads i1 element]
  [in-cx [#
[+ $x 2]
#]]
  [out-cx [#
[+ $x 2]
#]]
  [out-hash [#
sha2-256:7478d6bbf1e9ba55b778993530972b389ac28c2148a084b7460d2815d559d01c
#]]
 ]

