 [; ── #976: the OPERATOR form and its QUOTED-STRING twin are DISTINCT ──
    THE REGRESSION THIS FAMILY EXISTS TO CATCH. The document [<= 5 3] used
    to canonicalize into the one-item array of the string, byte-identical to
    the document that IS that array — and therefore to the same Tier-1 hash.
    Two documents the evaluator reads differently, false versus a text item,
    shared ONE content address. That is a content-addressing SOUNDNESS break,
    not a formatting wart. hash-eq FALSE is the property; if any of these
    ever flips to true the addressing scheme is broken again. ]

 [case id=oph-301-less-or-equal-operator-vs-string-distinct level=core
  [tags operator-heads issue-976 collision negative hash-pair]
  [in-a [#
[<= 5 3]
#]]
  [in-b [#
['<= 5 3']
#]]
  [out-hash-eq [#
false
#]]
 ]

 [case id=oph-302-percent-operator-vs-string-distinct level=core
  [tags operator-heads issue-976 collision negative hash-pair]
  [in-a [#
[% 7 3]
#]]
  [in-b [#
['% 7 3']
#]]
  [out-hash-eq [#
false
#]]
 ]

 [case id=oph-303-not-equals-operator-vs-string-distinct level=core
  [tags operator-heads issue-976 collision negative hash-pair]
  [in-a [#
[!= 5 3]
#]]
  [in-b [#
['!= 5 3']
#]]
  [out-hash-eq [#
false
#]]
 ]

 [; ── #976: guard rails — the sigils the widened alphabet brushes past ──
    `!` still opens a DECLARATION unless it is the delimited `!=` head, and
    a GLUED continuation still routes to the array lane. These are the
    dispatch neighbours the two-char admissions had to thread between; the
    `*` alias and negative-number guards are oph-008 and oph-009 above. ]

 [case id=oph-401-entity-declaration-unmoved level=core
  [tags operator-heads issue-976 guard declaration]
